<section class="panel" aria-label="Introduction to Addition Algorithms first-party visual mathematics">
  <h2>Warm-up — Which One Doesn’t Belong?</h2>
  <p>One value differs, and other options can be singled out by representation. Choose an option and explain why; the teacher accepts different well-supported reasons.</p>
  <ol><li>A: 200 + 30 + 17</li><li>B: 247</li><li>C: 200 + 47 + 10</li><li>D: 100 + 140 + 7</li></ol>
  <h2>Activity 1 — Explain the Algorithms</h2>
  <p>Tyler used a base-ten block drawing; Lin and Han used written records for 362 + 354. The following displays are independently typeset descriptions of the arithmetic, not publisher image reproduction. With a partner, compare each to your own base-ten drawing.</p>
  <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:12px">
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Lin: expanded addends</h3><p>300 + 60 + 2</p><p>300 + 50 + 4</p><p>600 + 110 + 6 = 716</p></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Han: partial-sum rows</h3><p>Ones: 2 + 4 = 6</p><p>Tens: 60 + 50 = 110</p><p>Hundreds: 300 + 300 = 600</p><p>Total: 6 + 110 + 600 = 716</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>Explain why the same place-value units are combined, even if the order and written layout differ. Make a visual display and compare approaches with MLR7 teacher-led discussion.</p>
  <div role="img" aria-label="Independent place value visual for 362 plus 354: six hundreds, eleven tens, six ones, equal 716" style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;padding:12px;border-radius:8px;margin-top:10px"><h3>First-party base-ten value diagram</h3><p>Hundreds: 300 + 300 = 600. Tens: 60 + 50 = 110. Ones: 2 + 4 = 6.</p><p>Six hundreds + eleven tens + six ones = 716. Exchange ten tens for one hundred to obtain seven hundreds, one ten and six ones.</p><p>This is a new textual model, not Tyler's publisher illustration.</p></div><h2>Activity 2 — Try an Algorithm</h2>
  <p>For each, choose a valid written method and show the value of the ones, tens and hundreds. Partners may use base-ten blocks.</p>
  <ol><li>475 + 231</li><li>136 + 389</li><li>670 + 257</li></ol>
  <p>With 670 + 257: 0 + 7 = 7, 70 + 50 = 120, and 600 + 200 = 800. Explain how the recorded partial sums make 927. Critique an incomplete explanation by adding first/next/then and identifying regrouped units (MLR3). The teacher reviews this critique.</p>
  <h2>Synthesis</h2>
  <p>What is an algorithm? How do Lin and Han agree and differ? An algorithm is an orderly repeatable method, not a claim that an entered number proves the original process work.</p>
